# Interview — 2026-09-14

Supervisor log for today: `Inbox/status-log/2026-09-14.md`

## 1. Orientation — What did you spend time on today?

Helped football collect jumps, then made the rounds with the athletic training staff. Went to a student services staff meeting. Then tried to map out and get a better handle on the projects I've actually been working on recently — the ones that should stay in the inventory because they move the needle. Getting closer to figuring that out. They center around three initiatives: **education, analytics, and research**.

## 2. Signal — What surprised you, frustrated you, or changed your mind?

Not surprised today. Slowly come to the realization that I take on too many projects and don't really finish anything before I attempt the next one. I need a system to prioritize my work. Because I'm largely self-directed, I'd benefit greatly from a true process to follow.

Asked for help building it. Tried before, but in a better place now to build a system and a routine. The meetings with Kenny and Chris Ruf, plus the updated job description, already outline some of this. Idea: OKRs built around the primary areas I'll be evaluated on that also move the needle in the three pillars (education, analytics, research).

## 3. Pattern — Connections to recent thinking?

Surfaced three earlier notes on the same problem: [[self-authored-direction]] (5/22), [[the-daily-on-ramp]] (6/2), [[keep-the-main-thing-the-main-thing]] (7/31).

Answer: It does connect. Been on a long pursuit to improve productivity and figure out how to provide value. Little success so far.

Not answered (carried to follow-up): what made the earlier fixes stop working, and why now is different.

## 4. Essay seed — One thing you'd tell a peer about today?

"Finish one project before you start the next one."

---

